# Email Validation on Signup Form

Here's the laziest working solution — use HTML5's native email validation:

```html
<form id="signup">
  <input type="email" name="email" required>
  <button type="submit">Sign Up</button>
</form>

<script>
  document.getElementById('signup').addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
    const email = document.querySelector('input[type="email"]');
    if (!email.checkValidity()) {
      e.preventDefault();
      alert('Invalid email');
    }
  });
</script>
```

**If you need stronger validation** (the browser's regex is loose), add JS:

```javascript
const validateEmail = (email) => /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email);

form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  if (!validateEmail(email.value)) {
    e.preventDefault();
    alert('Enter a valid email');
  }
});
```

**If this is backend** (Node/Python/etc.), use the same regex or just try sending a confirmation email — that's the real validation.

Skipped: fancy libraries, RFC 5322 parser, DNS checks. Add when you need to reject addresses that *look* valid but don't exist (confirmation email is cheaper).
